{"page_content": "9 \n \n \n \nFigure 5. Effect of helium dilution on cool flame extinction during n-dodecane droplet combustion. \n \n \n5.4  Sooting behavior as function of ambient pressure: \nThe effects and the process of soot formation during cool flame combustion remains largely unexplored. The \nshoot shell that forms during hot flame is stabilized by the balance of force between fluid mechanical drag \nand the thermophoretic force. At low pressures a stable spherical soot-shell is formed. However, as the \npressure increase the soot-shell becomes diffuse and undefined.  The reasons for this phenomenon is still \nnot well understood. At low pressures, due to the low flame temperature of the cool flame, soot is not \nformed. The soot that is formed during the hot flame combustion is simply convected away from the droplet \nsurface. But, when the pressure increases, residual soot is seen surrounding the droplet even after the hot \nflame extinction.  The mechanisms behind this process has not been fully understood, and further analysis \nand research is needed.", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 10}}